mechanics.text:
Victory Points are kept with VP Tokens as they are gained (5.2.4), and the End Phase is where the Tasks are checked and paid (3.7.3). A standard game lasts five rounds. After the fifth, the game ends and both sides total their VP: the side with more wins. On a tie, the side with more Mech Parts and Drones remaining on the board wins. Low Value Drones are not part of the squad, so they do not count (p.82).

Wiping out the enemy is not a win on the spot (FAQ P21). The game still runs its rounds, and the player with units left plays on alone to score more. A player may concede instead, and the other side then wins.

The one early end is VIP: Assassination. When a Commander is destroyed, the game ends at that round's End Phase, once the round's Victory Points and every end-of-game line are paid, and the side with more VP wins. If both Commanders fall in the same round, both sides score their 10.

VP come from:
- the Main Task, which both sides play;
- each player's own Secondary Task;
- two Parts that print a VP line: the OCS85 Black Box Carrying Pack (Black Box Main Task only) and the HD-2 Data Backpack (on an Escort target only, FAQ P22).

Who made a kill (FAQ P4):
- a Melee or Firing Action: the attacker;
- a Projectile's Explosion: the Projectile, so its launcher earns no Weapons Test point;
- a Mech removed for Integrity Loss: the last unit that destroyed one of its Parts;
- a unit destroyed by its own side: that side's own kill, which never costs the other player Mercy.

Destroying a Low Value unit never scores (p.82). The same unit may be the target of several Tasks at once (FAQ P13), and a named target pays its Task however it is destroyed, by its own side included.

At the start of the Command Phase every Mech generates 1 face-up Command Token on its Torso card, or X if the Torso prints Command Generation X (3.2.1). A Mech in Shutdown still generates its default token (ruled 2026-09-25).

The GoF Command cards are read at their 1.021 text (ruling I1):
- the Integrated Data Link Pod gives Command Generation +1;
- the P24 Chariot's Command Coordination may issue up to 2 Commands;
- the P28 Dragoon may perform one Command Coordination after a Maneuver;
- the P7 Warrior's Swarm Tactics: after it commands a GoF Medium Drone, the same token may go on at once to another Drone that has had no Command this Phase, before the other squad issues, and on again from each GoF Medium Drone it reaches (ruled 2026-09-28);
- the Firewatch pilot may consume 1 Link to generate a Command Token as its Mech gains an Action Opportunity.

The players alternate giving Commands (3.2.3), and a player who passes forfeits every remaining Command that phase (FAQ A21). Issuing one takes a token from a Mech of your choice and lays it face-down on an Ally Drone, which immediately moves or performs one Command Action. A Command Token is one token with two faces, face-up while it is available and face-down once used (2.5.1). A Drone holds only one token and takes one Command per Phase (FAQ N8, O1). The Drones' tokens are removed when the Command Phase ends.

A Mech's unspent tokens stay on it. An Action with Command Coordination X hands up to X of them to X different Drones later in the round, each acting at once as on a Command (4.15.3); a Drone that already acted in the Command Phase may take one. An Action that consumes a Command Token flips it face-down (4.15.4). Additional Instructions lets a Drone take one more Command Action without a token, and the Drone keeps its own Command (FAQ O14). The End Phase removes every Command Token wherever it lies.

Setup runs in this order (FAQ P1, 3.1):
- agree the battlefield. Environment Cards, if used, go down now, alternately, no more than the Battlefield Card allows (5.4.1);
- roll for First Player;
- fix the Main Task. Its card shows both Deployment Zones (5.2.3);
- the First Player chooses the Black or White side (3.1.2), knowing now where both zones lie;
- the First Player chooses and reveals a Secondary Task, then the other player does (3.1.3);
- place the Task Items and name the Task targets, alternately from the First Player (5.2.1);
- deploy, then start round 1.

Both players roll 2 yellow dice, and more Hits makes that side First Player (3.1.2). A hollow Hit counts nothing. The book gives no tie rule; the app has both players roll again.

The First Player deploys one unit wholly within an unoccupied Grid of its Deployment Zone. The players then alternate one unit at a time, and once one side has placed everything, the other places all its remaining units (3.1.4). A Grid here is a Large Grid, so each holds one unit, and terrain in it does not matter if the base fits. If every Grid of the zone is taken, the rest go in the Grids next to the zone's edge, working outward (FAQ P23).

What may be deployed:
- A Mech needs at least a Torso, a Chassis and one Arm, and must be assigned a Pilot; the other Parts may be left out (2.2.2, 5.1, FAQ P14, P16).
- A Mech picks its Stance as it lands. One found without a Stance Token is Offensive.
- A unit able to activate Optical Camouflage may deploy already in it (FAQ I6).
- Projectiles and Low Value units are never placed at deployment (p.82).
- The opponent may look over a deployed Mech's cards, except Covert Parts (3.1.4).
